---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_vi4_480",
  "series": "Band VI 4 (1677 - 1690)",
  "seq": 480,
  "text": "[[2123.15]] <b>385₁.  Erster Ansatz Januar 1677 ERSTER ANSATZ </p>\n<p> [[2123.16]] Januar 1677 </p>\n<p> [[2123.17]] Excussi <Fn><i>Auf der Rückseite</i>: Bellarminus ait Chrysostomum docere Purgatorium inprimis <i>Homil. 3. in Epist. ad Philipp.</i> et <i>Homil. 69. ad populum Antiochen.</i> Augustinus lib. 12 <i>Genes.</i> c. 33 de [<i>bricht ab</i>]</Fn> loca plurima veterum quae a Romanae Ecclesiae scriptoribus ad probandum\n[[2123.18]] purgatorium reperiuntur, et rejectis illis quae mihi non magnam vim habere videntur,\n[[2123.19]] illa saltem retuli quae suspicionem aliquam injicere possunt Sententiae a Romana\n[[2123.20]] hodierna non abhorrentis. </p>\n<p> [[2123.21]] Ex discussione autem ista hoc saltem didici: Omnes fere sanctos Patres ignem\n[[2123.22]] quendam purgatorium seu poenam temporalem post mortem etiam fidelibus plerisque\n[[2124.1]] infligendam statuisse, graviorem pro modo peccatorum. Eo tamen discrimine ut Graecae\n[[2124.2]] Ecclesiae doctores id quidem ad ignem illum transitus retulerint, quem sentire omnes ipso\n[[2124.3]] resurrectionis tempore necesse erit cum in occursum Domini rapientur, quanquam huic\n[[2124.4]] quoque igni moram aliquam concessisse videantur. Ecclesia occidentalis jam a Tertulliano\n[[2124.5]] et Cypriano eo inclinasse videtur, ut statueret, animam statim a morte poenis\n[[2124.6]] quibusdam subjici, et ex iis purgatam liberari atque ad beatitudinem admitti. Negari\n[[2124.7]] itaque non potest ejus sententia quae nunc in Romana viget Ecclesia, expressa satis etiam\n[[2124.8]] in vetere Latina vestigia extitisse. In Graeca vero Ecclesia neque olim, neque ut arbitror\n[[2124.9]] hodie ejusmodi purgatorium creditum est. </p>\n<p> [[2124.10]] Ex locis veterum quae ad probandum purgatorium afferuntur, paucissima ad rem\n[[2124.11]] pertinent. Ecclesia Romana hodie docet, <b>Purgatorium</b> esse sive locum sive statum\n[[2124.12]] animarum fidelium, in quo post mortem aliquandiu poenas peccatorum gravissimas luunt,\n[[2124.13]] et vivorum precibus ac sacrificiis juvantur: donec placata divina justitia purgatae ad\n[[2124.14]] beatitudinem admittuntur. </p>\n<p> [[2124.15]] Loca autem quae ad hoc probandum afferuntur ex Sanctis Patribus, <b>alia</b> tantum\n[[2124.16]] probant pro mortuis esse orandum, <b>alia</b> tantum ostendunt aliquod esse purgatorium. Hoc\n[[2124.17]] vero multi veteres ac recentiores admittunt tempore resurrectionis, ubi omnes per ignem\n[[2124.18]] probandos arbitrantur, neque ideo purgatorium Romanae Ecclesiae agnoscunt. </p>\n<p> [[2124.19]] Fateor, <b>ex oratione pro defunctis non sequi purgatorium Romanae\n[[2124.20]] Ecclesiae</i>. Vis argumenti talis: Si orandum est pro mortuis, sequitur mortuos precibus\n[[2124.21]] juvari. Si mortui precibus juvantur, sequitur eos neque plene damnatos, neque omnino\n[[2124.22]] salvos, sed adhuc poenae capaces esse. Si mortui fideles poenae capaces sunt, sequitur\n[[2124.23]] esse Purgatorium. Ergo si pro mortuis orandum est, admittendum est purgatorium. </p>\n<p> [[2124.24]] Ad hoc argumentum responderi potest <b>primum negando, quod necesse sit\n[[2124.25]] mortuos precibus juvari.</i> At vero, inquies, ita inutiles sunt preces. Fateor inutiles\n[[2124.26]] erunt ipsis mortuis, sed non omnino omnibus aliis. Erunt enim preces pro defunctis,\n[[2124.27]] saltem vota quaedam. Vota autem etiam tum concipi possunt et solent, cum res non est\n[[2124.28]] amplius integra; usumque saltem hunc habent, tum ut affectum nostrum testemur, tum ut\n[[2124.29]] omnia ad Dei, quem imploramus, voluntatem referamus, quodam honoris, et cultus\n[[2125.1]] genere. Ita solemus de rebus praeteritis vota et tantum non preces concipere. Pone\n[[2125.2]] nuntiari mihi praelium ingens, cui frater meus aut amicus interfuit. Ego statim ad Deum\n[[2125.3]] ac vota conversus ex animo opto, ut ne quid mali ei acciderit: quanquam postea re\n[[2125.4]] subtilius excussa facile intelligam factum infectum fieri non posse, et, si ille semel\n[[2125.5]] occisus est non ideo non-occisum, si salvus evasit non ideo hodie in praelio hesterno\n[[2125.6]] occisum iri. Nescio quid alii faciant, mihi certe saepe ejusmodi vota primo animi impetu\n[[2125.7]] excidere. Fortasse non defuere qui et nuncupavere vota in casu simili, et damnati solvere.\n[[2125.8]] Quo minus mirandum est viventes in vota erumpere pro mortuis, caritate accensos,\n[[2125.9]] quoniam de re summa animae nimirum defuncti felicitate, agitur. Nam incerta atque\n[[2125.10]] ignota a nobis solent pro infectis haberi, quae vera est ratio cur circa res praeteritas ac\n[[2125.11]] transactas preces fundamus. Idem est de futuris certis ac determinatis. Nemo dubitat quin\n[[2125.12]] beatitudo aut damnatio mortuorum certa sit, neque ullis precibus ac meritis mutabilis,\n[[2125.13]] nam ex Romanae quoque Ecclesiae sententia, si damnati sunt, certe ex inferno non\n[[2125.14]] redimentur, si in coelum translati, non excident felicitate; si in purgatorio sunt, securi sunt\n[[2125.15]] felicitatis futurae. Mortuorum ergo beatitudo ac damnatio nulli mutationi subjecta est.\n[[2125.16]] Inutilia ergo ipsis erunt vota quibus a Deo aeternam eorum salutem flagitabimus. Usui\n[[2125.17]] tamen et instinctui cuidam naturali convenire nemo diffitebitur. </p>\n<p> [[2125.18]] Deinde <b>negatur ex eo quod mortui precibus juvantur, sequi eos sub\n[[2125.19]] poenis esse</i>; possunt enim majorem gloriam nostris precibus obtinere. </p>\n<p> [[2125.20]] <b>Tertio negatur ex eo quod puniuntur fideles mortui, sequi Purgatorium\n[[2125.21]] Romanae Ecclesiae</i>, Graeci enim Veteres id de suo purgatorio interpretabantur,\n[[2125.22]] quod in die resurrectionis omnes subire arbitrantur. </p>\n<p> [[2125.23]] Et haec quidem de Locis in quibus fit mentio precum pro mortuis; <b>accedo ad\n[[2125.24]] loca in quibus fit mentio ipsius poenae temporalis post mortem, ignis,\n[[2125.25]] purgationis.</i> Talia sunt loca quod omnis anima igne salvetur; quod illi quorum opus\n[[2125.26]] arserit salvi erunt quasi per ignem, quod (: argumento a contrario sensu :) quaedam\n[[2125.27]] peccata in futuro seculo remittentur. Haec omnia commode admodum explicari possunt\n[[2125.28]] per purgatorium resurrectionis. </p>\n",
  "number": "385₁.",
  "title": "Erster Ansatz Januar 1677",
  "date": null
};
---

<Layout title={`${entry.title || entry.id} - Leibniz Writings`}>
  <article data-pagefind-body>
    <header class="entry-header">
      <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">{entry.title || entry.id}</h1>

      <div class="entry-meta">
        {entry.date && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Date:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="date">{entry.date}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Series:</strong>
          <span data-pagefind-meta="series">{entry.series}</span>
        </div>

        {entry.number && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Number:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="number">{entry.number}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Sequence:</strong>
          <span>{entry.seq}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- View Toggle -->
    <div class="view-controls">
      <button id="translation-view-btn" class="view-btn active">Translation View</button>
      <button id="scholarly-view-btn" class="view-btn">Scholarly View</button>
    </div>

    <div class="entry-content" data-pagefind-meta="excerpt:385₁. Erster Ansatz Januar 1677 ERSTER ANSATZ Januar 1677 Excussi Auf der Rückseite: Bellarminus ait Chrysostomum docere Purgatorium inprimis Homil. 3. in Epist. ad Philipp. et Homil. 69. ad populum Antiochen. Augustinus lib. 12 Genes. c. 33 de [bricht ab] loca plurima veterum quae a Romanae Ecclesi...">

      <!-- Translation-friendly continuous text -->
      <div id="translation-view" class="text-view active">
        <div class="translation-notice">
          <p><strong>📖 Translation View:</strong> Continuous text optimized for browser translation tools.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="continuous-text" translate="yes" lang="unknown" id="continuous-text">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
        <div class="line-numbers-reference" id="line-numbers-ref">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Detailed scholarly view with line numbers -->
      <div id="scholarly-view" class="text-view">
        <div class="scholarly-notice">
          <p><strong>📚 Scholarly View:</strong> Original text with line number annotations preserved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="annotated-text" id="annotated-text">
          {entry.text.split('\n').map(paragraph =>
            paragraph.trim() && <p set:html={paragraph} />
          )}
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

  <nav class="entry-navigation">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to Search</a>
  </nav>
</Layout>

<style>
  .entry-header {
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
  }

  .entry-header h1 {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .entry-meta {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .meta-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .meta-item strong {
    color: var(--text);
    min-width: 80px;
  }

  .meta-item span {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    flex: 1;
  }

  .entry-content {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .text-content {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 30px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .text-content :global(p) {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .text-content :global(p:last-child) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .text-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .entry-navigation {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 20px;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--primary-dark);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* View Toggle Controls */
  .view-controls {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .view-btn {
    background: transparent;
    border: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    transition: all 0.2s;
  }

  .view-btn:hover {
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .view-btn.active {
    background: var(--primary);
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: white;
  }

  /* Text Views */
  .text-view {
    display: none;
  }

  .text-view.active {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Translation View */
  .translation-notice, .scholarly-notice {
    background: var(--background);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .continuous-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 2px solid var(--primary);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 25px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 15px;
  }

  .line-numbers-reference {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 13px;
    padding: 10px;
    background: var(--background);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* Scholarly View */
  .annotated-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .text-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .line-refs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .line-num {
    background: var(--primary);
    color: white;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-family: monospace;
  }

  .line-content {
    flex: 1;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .line-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .line-content :global(i) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .entry-meta {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .view-controls {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .text-line {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 6px;
    }

    .line-refs {
      min-width: auto;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Process text for translation view
  function processTextForTranslation(text) {
    if (!text) return { translationText: '', lineNumbers: [] };

    const lines = text.split('\n');
    let translationText = '';
    const lineNumbers = [];

    lines.forEach(line => {
      const trimmedLine = line.trim();
      if (!trimmedLine) return;

      // Extract line numbers
      const lineNumberMatch = trimmedLine.match(/\[\[(\d+\.\d+)\]\]/g);
      const extractedNumbers = lineNumberMatch ?
        lineNumberMatch.map(m => m.replace(/\[|\]/g, '')) : [];

      // Remove line number markers and HTML tags for continuous text
      let contentOnly = trimmedLine
        .replace(/\[\[\d+\.\d+\]\]/g, '')
        .replace(/<\/?[^>]+(>|$)/g, '')
        .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
        .trim();

      if (contentOnly) {
        if (translationText) translationText += ' ';
        translationText += contentOnly;
        lineNumbers.push(...extractedNumbers);
      }
    });

    return {
      translationText: translationText.trim(),
      lineNumbers: lineNumbers
    };
  }

  // View switching and text processing
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const translationBtn = document.getElementById('translation-view-btn');
    const scholarlyBtn = document.getElementById('scholarly-view-btn');
    const translationView = document.getElementById('translation-view');
    const scholarlyView = document.getElementById('scholarly-view');
    const continuousTextDiv = document.getElementById('continuous-text');
    const lineNumbersRef = document.getElementById('line-numbers-ref');

    // Process the text for translation view
    if (continuousTextDiv && lineNumbersRef && entry && entry.text) {
      const processed = processTextForTranslation(entry.text);
      continuousTextDiv.textContent = processed.translationText;

      if (processed.lineNumbers.length > 0) {
        lineNumbersRef.innerHTML = `<small><strong>Line numbers:</strong> ${processed.lineNumbers.join(', ')}</small>`;
      }
    }

    // View switching functionality
    if (translationBtn && scholarlyBtn && translationView && scholarlyView) {
      translationBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        translationBtn.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyBtn.classList.remove('active');
        translationView.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.remove('active');
      });

      scholarlyBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        scholarlyBtn.classList.add('active');
        translationBtn.classList.remove('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.add('active');
        translationView.classList.remove('active');
      });
    }
  });
</script>